+# tidyjson
+
+A small command-line tool that reformats a JSON file in place.
+
+## What it does
+
+`tidyjson` reads a JSON file, re-serializes it with consistent indentation (and optionally sorted keys), and writes the result back to the same file.
+
+If the file is not valid JSON, `tidyjson` exits with status `2` and leaves the file untouched.
+
+## Installation
+
+Requires [Node.js](https://nodejs.org/).
+
+From a clone of this repository:
+
+```sh
+npm install -g .
+```
+
+This puts the `tidyjson` command on your `PATH`. You can also run it without installing:
+
+```sh
+node cli.js <file> [options]
+```
+
+## Usage
+
+```sh
+tidyjson <file> [--indent N] [--sort-keys]
+```
+
+| Argument / option | Description |
+| --- | --- |
+| `<file>` | Path to the JSON file to reformat. The file is overwritten in place. |
+| `--indent N` | Indent nested levels by `N` spaces. |
+| `--sort-keys` | Sort object keys alphabetically. |
+
+### Examples
+
+Reformat a file with 2-space indentation:
+
+```sh
+tidyjson config.json --indent 2
+```
+
+Reformat with 4-space indentation and sorted keys:
+
+```sh
+tidyjson package-lock.json --indent 4 --sort-keys
+```
+
+Check for invalid JSON in a script:
+
+```sh
+tidyjson data.json
+if [ $? -eq 2 ]; then
+  echo "data.json is not valid JSON"
+fi
+```
+
+## Exit codes
+
+| Code | Meaning |
+| --- | --- |
+| `0` | The file was reformatted. |
+| `2` | The file is not valid JSON. The file was not modified. |
+
+## Notes
+
+- `tidyjson` modifies the file in place. Commit or back up the file first if you might want the original formatting back.
+
+## Development
+
+Run the tests with:
+
+```sh
+npm test
+```
+
+This uses Node's built-in test runner (`node --test`).
